'''Simulated reality''' is the hypothesis or thought experiment that an experienced world may be generated by a computational or other simulation. It differs from ordinary virtual reality, where users know they are interacting with an engineered representation.

== Distinct hypotheses ==
A local simulation models selected aspects of a system; an immersive virtual environment presents generated sensory input to an external user; the simulated-reality hypothesis proposes that the observers or their experienced world are themselves realized through a simulation. These are distinct claims. Classical skeptical scenarios such as dreams or brains in vats raise related questions about knowledge, but do not require modern digital computing.

== Bostrom's simulation argument ==
Bostrom (2003) argues that, given specified assumptions, at least one of three propositions holds: civilizations like ours rarely reach a posthuman stage, such civilizations rarely run many ancestor simulations, or observers like us are overwhelmingly likely to be simulated. The argument is conditional on assumptions about substrate-independent consciousness, technological feasibility, numbers of observers and observer-selection reasoning. It is not a direct measurement of the probability that this world is simulated.

In a deliberately simplified counting model with N_s simulated observers and N_b non-simulated observers in a chosen reference class, a uniform sampling assumption gives <math>P(S)=N_s/(N_s+N_b)</math>. The result changes if the reference class, observer weighting or assumptions about consciousness change. Neither population count is empirically known. The formula is an illustration of selection reasoning rather than a calibrated probability estimate.

== Testability and scientific limits ==
A restricted simulation model could make a distinctive prediction if it specifies implementation constraints that entail observable effects not shared by competing physical models. Such a prediction must be evaluated against ordinary mechanisms, measurement error and alternative explanations. A simulator allowed to reproduce any observation is not distinguishable by those observations alone. Apparent coincidences, numerical patterns or computational descriptions of physical laws do not independently establish a simulated world. The hypothesis remains philosophical and speculative in the absence of discriminating evidence; it provides no basis for treating ordinary physical risks or other people as unreal.

